---
/**
 * The monthly charge — the button that puts a shop on the site, and the one that stops paying for it.
 *
 * ── Why it is on the Payments tab and not in a wizard ──
 * It is the second half of the same errand as the two blocks above it: the bank account says where
 * the seller's money lands, the business details are what the processor examines, and this is what
 * he pays us. One screen owns money (owner, 2026-08-23).
 *
 * ── One charge, several shops ──
 * Since 2026-08-24 a plan is bought per shop and the seller has ONE standing order whose amount is
 * the sum of the shops he has on the site (`lib/store-plan.ts`). So this card has to answer two
 * different questions without mixing them: *what does THIS shop cost*, and *what is being charged to
 * my card in total*. The breakdown is rendered only when there is more than one line — with one
 * shop the total IS the plan, and printing it twice is how a screen starts looking complicated.
 *
 * ── The button leads OFF-SITE, and the copy says so ──
 * PayMe return their own payment page unless we hand them a card token, and we cannot: Hosted Fields
 * need the merchant's `seller_public_key` and **the merchant here is ours**, whose key was never
 * stored (`seller-subscription.ts`). Pretending otherwise — a spinner, then a redirect the seller
 * did not expect — is how a payment gets abandoned halfway. So the sentence under the button names
 * where it goes.
 *
 * ── Cancelling is a two-step, and the middle step is not a dark pattern ──
 * The owner asked for retention (*"כמובן צריך לנסות לשמר את הלקוח"*). What is offered is the two
 * things that are actually cheaper than leaving and that he may not know exist: a lower plan, and —
 * with more than one shop — taking one shop off the site instead of all of them. Both are real
 * alternatives to the thing he pressed. There is no "are you sure" loop and no offer he has to
 * decline twice: the cancel button is right there, in the same panel, at the same size.
 *
 * The sentence that does most of the work is the one about WHEN: he keeps everything to the end of
 * the month he already paid for (owner, 2026-08-24). A seller who knows that is a seller who is not
 * cancelling in a hurry to avoid being charged again.
 */
import { resolveTier, SELLER_SETUP_FEE, feeWithVatAgorot, type SellerTierId } from '../../lib/pricing.js';
import PlanPills from './PlanPills.astro';
import { formatAgorot, fromAgorot, toAgorot } from '../../lib/money.js';
import { chargesVat, platformVatPercent } from '../../lib/vat.js';
import { CANCEL_REASONS, cancelReasonKey } from '../../lib/subscription-cancel.js';
import type { getT } from '../../i18n/index.js';
import { isDemoMode } from '../../lib/demo-mode.js';

interface Props {
  /** The shop this card is about — every action it takes names it, because the plan and the line on
   *  the monthly charge belong to the shop rather than to the account. */
  storeId: string;
  /** This shop's plan. Absent falls back to the default, exactly as `resolveTier` does everywhere
   *  else — a bad stored value must never break this render. */
  tier: SellerTierId | undefined;
  /** What the plan is CALLED. It lives in the pricing copy rather than the dashboard's, so it is
   *  handed in rather than looked up here — this card would otherwise be the one component that
   *  reaches into another screen's translations to render a word.
   *
   *  Named at all because a seller who chose "צמיחה" met the word nowhere afterwards: the card said
   *  "125 ₪ לחודש" and left him to recognise his plan by its price (owner, 2026-08-24: *"מה קורה
   *  אחרי שעושים בחירת מסלול? איפה זה מופיע?"*). */
  planName: string;
  /** What every plan is called, for the chooser. Handed in for the same reason `planName` is: the
   *  words live in the pricing copy, and a dashboard component that reaches into another screen's
   *  translations is the one that goes stale when that screen is rewritten. */
  planNames: Record<SellerTierId, string>;
  /** What is on file: `null` = none yet, `paying` = billing is running, otherwise it was created and
   *  nobody has paid it. `endsAt` is present only on a cancelled one still inside its paid period. */
  subscription: {
    status: number | null;
    nextCharge?: string;
    endsAt?: string;
    priceAgorot?: number;
    storeFees?: { storeId: string; storeName: string; tier: string; feeAgorot: number }[];
    /** When the card was put on file. The only fact about it we hold — no PAN is ever stored — and
     *  it is what makes "a card is on file" a statement rather than a reassurance. */
    cardSavedAt?: string;
  } | null;
  paying: boolean;
  /** Whether THIS shop is one of the lines in the standing order. A paying seller who has just
   *  opened a second shop is `paying: true` and `storeInPlan: false` — the case where the card must
   *  offer to ADD a shop rather than to start a subscription. */
  storeInPlan?: boolean;
  /** A card is on file and nothing has been charged: he chose during PayMe's review and the first
   *  charge fires when the shop goes up (`lib/subscription-arm.ts`). */
  cardOnFile?: boolean;
  /** Who the card belongs to. PayMe's tokenizer wants a payer, and these are the seller's own
   *  account details — not a form for him to fill in again (`feedback_seller_form_burden`).
   *
   *  **`phone` is not optional to them.** The script has always passed one and this component never
   *  rendered it, so every tokenisation went out with an empty string and came back refused
   *  (owner, 2026-08-25: *"הכרטיס לא התקבל"*). It comes from the clearing details he has already
   *  given — `merchant_kyc.ownerPhone` — which is why the card usually asks him for nothing.
   *
   *  **Usually, not always, and that was the second half of the same bug.** Step 3 is open from the
   *  first minute on purpose (`GoLiveSteps.astro`: the plan is ours and the card is charged to OUR
   *  merchant, so it waits on PayMe for nothing) — which means a seller who has typed nothing into
   *  step 1 reaches these fields with no phone anywhere, and the refusal came back as PayMe's own
   *  merchant-facing English: *"Please provide \"payerPhone\" value"* (owner, 2026-08-26). Blocking
   *  the card behind ten KYC fields would undo exactly what step 3 being open buys, so when we hold
   *  no phone the card asks for that ONE field and `/api/seller/subscription` writes it into
   *  `merchant_kyc.ownerPhone` — so step 1 comes back with it already filled in, and the number a
   *  seller gives can never be two different numbers. */
  payer: { name: string; email: string; phone: string };
  /** The seller's registered business type — `exempt` | `licensed` | `company`, or nothing yet.
   *
   *  **It decides one line and nothing else** (owner, 2026-08-26). Our fees are quoted before VAT
   *  and an **עוסק פטור** cannot reclaim it, so for him the gross is the only figure that describes
   *  what leaves his account — *"שלעוסק פטור יהיה כתוב גם הסה״כ, ולעוסק מורשה ומעלה לא. בצורה
   *  עדינה."* `chargesVat` is the same test that decides whether HIS invoice to a buyer carries
   *  VAT, so the two can never disagree about what kind of business he is. Absent answers "cannot
   *  reclaim", for the reason `vat.ts` gives: we do not know, and the extra line is the safer of
   *  the two mistakes. */
  businessType?: string;
  /** Rendered INSIDE a go-live step, which already carries the title and the one-line reason. Drops
   *  its own heading and its own frame, so the step is one thing rather than a box in a box saying
   *  "מנוי חודשי" twice (owner, 2026-08-24). */
  bare?: boolean;
  d: ReturnType<typeof getT>['dashboard'];
}
const { storeId, tier, planName, planNames, subscription, paying, storeInPlan = false, cardOnFile = false, payer, businessType, bare = false, d } = Astro.props;

/** Read here rather than passed in: it is a fact about the SERVER, not about this card, and a
 *  prop would be one more thing every caller has to remember to thread through correctly. */
const demoMode = isDemoMode();
const plan = resolveTier(tier);
const created = !!subscription;
/** A cancelled subscription still inside the period he paid for. Not the same as "not paying": he
 *  is, until this date, and the card must say so rather than offering him to start again. */
const endingOn = subscription?.endsAt;
const lines = subscription?.storeFees ?? [];
/** Only worth showing when the total is not simply this shop's plan. */
const showBreakdown = paying && lines.length > 1;
/** Do we charge VAT at all? Our own status, not his (`vat.ts#platformVatPercent`) — an עוסק פטור
 *  platform bills net, and then neither the "+ מע״מ" line nor the gross beside it is true. */
const platformVat = platformVatPercent() > 0;
/** A seller who deducts no input tax, so the gross is the number that describes his month. Only
 *  meaningful while there IS a tax on the fee. */
const seesGross = platformVat && !chargesVat(businessType);
/** Is there a card on file at all — armed, or being charged? It decides three things at once: the
 *  card form opens collapsed, "ביטול" has somewhere to go back to, and the two first-charge
 *  sentences under the save button are suppressed. */
const hasCard = cardOnFile || (paying && storeInPlan);
const cardSavedAt = subscription?.cardSavedAt;
/** A reason's label, by its key. The cast is the narrow one this needs: `d` is a frozen literal
 *  type, so indexing it with a computed key is an error however real the key is — and computing it
 *  (`cancelReasonKey`) is the whole point, since it is what stops the id list and the copy from
 *  being maintained in step by hand. */
const reasonLabel = (reason: string): string =>
  (d as unknown as Record<string, string>)[cancelReasonKey(reason as never)] ?? '';
/** This shop's plan as it is really billed. Through `feeWithVatAgorot` and never `× 1.18` written
 *  out here: it is the same function the standing order's price goes through
 *  (`store-plan.ts#billedTotalAgorot`), so the sentence and the debit are one arithmetic. */
const planGrossAgorot = feeWithVatAgorot(toAgorot(plan.monthlyFee));
/** What the plans add up to before tax — the breakdown's own subtotal. `priceAgorot` is the charge,
 *  VAT included, so the difference between them is the VAT row and the three figures agree by
 *  construction rather than by two roundings happening to match. */
const linesNetAgorot = lines.reduce((sum, l) => sum + l.feeAgorot, 0);
const vatAgorot = Math.max(0, (subscription?.priceAgorot ?? 0) - linesNetAgorot);
---

<section class={bare ? '' : 'card card--flat p-4 md:p-5 mt-4'} id="sub-card" data-paying={String(paying)} data-store={storeId}>
  {/* ── Choosing, at the moment it starts costing him ──
      First thing in the step, because the figure two rows below is the one it decides. Only in
      `bare`: on the standalone card a live shop is already ON a plan, and four pills at the top of
      a card whose subject is "your subscription is running" invites a decision nobody came to make.
      There it sits behind the switch button instead. */}
  {bare && (
    <div class="mb-3">
      <p class="m-0 text-[0.84rem] font-semibold mb-2">{d.glPlanTitle}</p>
      <PlanPills storeId={storeId} tier={tier} planNames={planNames} d={d} />
    </div>
  )}
  {!bare && (
    <>
      <h3 class="text-[1.05rem] font-bold m-0">{d.subTitle}</h3>
      <p class="muted m-0 mt-1 text-[0.84rem]">{d.subWhy}</p>
    </>
  )}

  {/* Label, colon, value, one per row — the shape facts take on this dashboard
      (`feedback_no_standing_screen_prose`), rather than a sentence a seller has to parse to find
      his own commission rate in. */}
  <dl class={`grid grid-cols-[auto_1fr] gap-x-3 gap-y-1 text-[0.86rem] m-0 ${bare ? '' : 'mt-4'}`}>
    {/* Dropped in `bare`, where the four plan pills sit directly above this grid and the marked one
        already carries the name and the price — repeating them a line later is the same fact twice
        in two shapes, which is what made the go-live step read as two cards. On the standalone card
        there are no pills, so the row is the only place the plan is named. */}
    {!bare && (
      <>
        <dt class="muted">{d.subPlan}</dt>
        <dd class="m-0 font-semibold">{d.subPlanValue.replace('{name}', planName).replace('{fee}', String(plan.monthlyFee)).replace('{per}', d.subPerMonth)}</dd>
      </>
    )}
    <dt class="muted">{d.subCommission}</dt>
    {/* Named so a plan change can repaint it without reloading the tab — it is the only figure on
        this card that the choice moves. */}
    <dd class="m-0 font-semibold" id="sub-commission">{plan.commissionPercent}%</dd>
    {showBreakdown && (
      <>
        {/* **The answer to "why am I charged ₪224".** One standing order, several shops — without
            this the seller reads a plan of ₪99 and a charge of ₪224 and has to guess at the
            arithmetic. Read from what the standing order was actually SET from, never re-derived,
            so this cannot disagree with his card statement. */}
        <dt class="muted">{d.subTotal}</dt>
        <dd class="m-0 font-semibold">{formatAgorot(subscription?.priceAgorot ?? 0)}</dd>
        {/* The VAT is a ROW of the breakdown rather than a note beside it, because without it the
            list of shops does not add up to the total above and the seller is left doing the
            arithmetic on a figure he is being charged (2026-08-26). */}
        {vatAgorot > 0 && (
          <>
            <dt class="muted">{d.subVatRow}</dt>
            <dd class="m-0 font-semibold">{formatAgorot(vatAgorot)}</dd>
          </>
        )}
        <dt class="muted">{d.subTotalOf}</dt>
        {/* `justify-self-start` and a width of its own, because the value column is `1fr`: a
            `justify-between` row inside it pushed every price to the far edge of the card, a hand's
            width from the shop it belongs to, with nothing in between. Measured at 1100px, where
            the three names ended around x=1430 and their ₪99s sat at x=60. The list is its own
            two-column grid so the prices still line up with each other. */}
        <dd class="m-0 justify-self-start w-full max-w-[22rem]">
          <ul role="list" class="list-none p-0 m-0 grid grid-cols-[1fr_auto] gap-x-4 gap-y-0.5">
            {lines.map((line) => (
              <>
                <span class={line.storeId === storeId ? 'font-semibold' : ''}>{line.storeName}</span>
                <span class="tabular-nums">{formatAgorot(line.feeAgorot)}</span>
              </>
            ))}
          </ul>
        </dd>
      </>
    )}
    {paying && !endingOn && subscription?.nextCharge && (
      <>
        <dt class="muted">{d.subNextCharge}</dt>
        {/* PayMe's own string, passed through unparsed — it is theirs, and re-formatting it is how
            a displayed date and a billed date start disagreeing. */}
        {/* Named, because the plan-change dialog reads the date off it — one figure on the page, so
            the sentence in the dialog and the row on the card cannot name two different days. */}
        <dd class="m-0 font-semibold justify-self-start" id="sub-next-charge" dir="ltr">{subscription.nextCharge}</dd>
      </>
    )}
  </dl>
  {/* Every number above is before VAT, and `pricing.ts`'s header argues at length why saying so is
      not optional: a seller who reads 99 and is invoiced 116.82 has been surprised by us on the one
      screen where trust is being established. */}
  {platformVat && <p class="muted m-0 mt-2 text-[0.76rem]">{d.subVat}</p>}
  {/* The second line, for a seller who cannot reclaim it. Same muted size and directly under the
      first — a statement, not a warning (owner: *"בצורה עדינה"*). Never rendered for an עוסק מורשה
      or a חברה, for whom it is noise about money that costs them nothing. */}
  {seesGross && (
    <p class="muted m-0 mt-0.5 text-[0.76rem]">{d.subVatTotal.replace('{amount}', formatAgorot(showBreakdown ? (subscription?.priceAgorot ?? 0) : planGrossAgorot))}</p>
  )}

  {/* ── Rendered in every state since 2026-08-26 (owner, סשן א׳ §9) ──
      *"לא רואה בכלל סקשן של אמצעי תשלום והחלפת אמצעי תשלום."* And there was none: this block was
      gated on `!paying || !storeInPlan`, so the moment a seller's subscription started running, the
      card form left the page — and with it the only way to replace an expiring card without
      cancelling the subscription. The one state where a seller is CERTAIN to need it was the one
      state that did not have it.

      It is `data-collapsed` whenever a card is already on file, so nothing changes on screen until
      "החלפת כרטיס" is pressed; `scripts/dashboard/subscription.ts` still mounts the iframes either
      way, because mounting them is what proves the SDK works at all. */}
  <div class:list={[cardOnFile ? 'mt-2' : 'mt-4']}>
      {/* ── PayMe's own card fields, drawn on OUR page ───────────────────────────────────────
          Three iframes served by PayMe and mounted here, so the seller commits at the moment he
          decides instead of a week later on somebody else's page. The container ids are the ones
          `scripts/checkout-card.ts` mounts into — the same three, because it is the same SDK call
          and duplicating them would be a second definition of a string that must match exactly.
          Hidden until the SDK answers: an empty bordered box where a card field should be is worse
          than no box at all, and `active: false` (no key configured) is a real state that falls
          back to PayMe's own page below. */}
      {/* What the tokenizer is told, carried as data rather than fetched again: the amount is the
          one this card already rendered, so the figure on the card issuer's own confirmation and
          the figure on this screen come from the same place. `firstName`/`lastName` is PayMe's
          split of a single Hebrew name — the first word and the rest, which is the honest reading
          of a name we hold as one string.

          `data-amount` is the GROSS — what the standing order really debits — because it is what
          the card issuer's own confirmation screen will show. Quoting the net there would put one
          number on the seller's phone and take another from the card (2026-08-26). */}
      <div
        class="!hidden mt-1"
        id="sub-card-fields"
        {...(hasCard ? { 'data-collapsed': 'true' } : {})}
        data-first-name={payer.name.trim().split(/\s+/)[0] ?? ''}
        data-last-name={payer.name.trim().split(/\s+/).slice(1).join(' ')}
        data-email={payer.email}
        data-phone={payer.phone}
        data-label={d.subTitle}
        data-amount={fromAgorot(planGrossAgorot).toFixed(2)}
      >
        {/* ── Fixed tracks, because these boxes contain IFRAMES ──
            An iframe has no intrinsic width to measure, so `auto` columns collapsed to nothing and
            `1fr` gave the rest away: the card number ended up a sliver with its label wrapped over
            two lines while תוקף and CVV took half the row each (owner's screenshot, 2026-08-25).
            Named tracks in `ch` instead — the card number keeps the row it needs, the two short
            fields take exactly what their content is, and nothing depends on measuring an empty
            frame. `minmax(0,…)` on the first so a long label cannot push the grid wider than its
            container.

            Each frame is given an explicit height too: an empty iframe is zero pixels tall until
            PayMe paint into it, which is what made the boxes different sizes from each other. */}
        {/* Ours, not PayMe's — the three boxes below are iframes and nothing can be put inside
            them. Rendered only while we hold no phone: a seller who filled step 1 has already
            answered this and asking twice is the burden `feedback_seller_form_burden` forbids.
            `inputmode="tel"` for the numeric keypad; the value is normalised server-side by
            `normalizeMerchantKyc`, which accepts +972 and landlines alike, so nothing here
            rejects a number the clearing form would have taken. */}
        {!payer.phone && (
          <div class="field m-0 mb-3 max-w-[22ch]">
            <span class="text-[0.8rem] whitespace-nowrap">{d.subCardPhone}</span>
            <input type="tel" inputmode="tel" autocomplete="tel" class="input h-[2.6rem]" id="sub-card-phone" name="subCardPhone" />
            <p class="muted m-0 mt-1 text-[0.78rem]">{d.subCardPhoneHint}</p>
          </div>
        )}
        <div class="grid gap-3 sm:grid-cols-[minmax(0,22ch)_9ch_7ch]">
          <div class="field m-0">
            <span class="text-[0.8rem] whitespace-nowrap">{d.subCardNumber}</span>
            <div id="payme-card-number" class="input flex items-center h-[2.6rem] py-0"></div>
          </div>
          <div class="field m-0">
            <span class="text-[0.8rem] whitespace-nowrap">{d.subCardExpiry}</span>
            <div id="payme-card-expiry" class="input flex items-center h-[2.6rem] py-0"></div>
          </div>
          <div class="field m-0">
            <span class="text-[0.8rem] whitespace-nowrap">{d.subCardCvc}</span>
            <div id="payme-card-cvc" class="input flex items-center h-[2.6rem] py-0"></div>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="flex flex-wrap items-center gap-2 mt-3">
          <button type="button" class="btn btn--accent btn--sm" id="sub-card-save" data-store={storeId}>{d.subCardSave}</button>
          {/* ── §16: the way back (owner, 2026-08-26) ──
              *"בעת החלפת הכרטיס, צריך שם אפשרות ביטול כדי להשאיר את הכרטיס הקיים על מקומו. כרגע אין
              דרך פשוט לחזור אחורה, זה כמו מסיר את הכרטיס."* Opening the form over a saved card left
              him staring at three empty boxes with no exit but a page reload, which reads as having
              thrown the card away. Rendered only when there IS a card to go back to — with none,
              closing the form would close it over nothing. */}
          {hasCard && <button type="button" class="btn btn--ghost btn--sm" id="sub-card-cancel">{d.payBankCancel}</button>}
        </div>
        {/* The sentence that makes the whole step acceptable, and it is the first thing under the
            button rather than the last thing on the card.

            Both lines are about the FIRST charge, so they are absent once there is a card being
            billed — on a REPLACEMENT they would promise a seller who is already paying that nothing
            will be taken, which is the opposite of true. */}
        {!hasCard && <p class="m-0 mt-2 text-[0.8rem] font-semibold">{d.subCardNoCharge}</p>}
        {!hasCard && <p class="muted m-0 mt-1 text-[0.78rem]">{d.subSetupFee.replace('{amount}', String(SELLER_SETUP_FEE))}</p>}
      </div>

  </div>

  <p class="hidden text-sm [color:var(--color-danger)] mt-3 mb-0" id="sub-error" role="alert"></p>

  {endingOn ? (
    /* Cancelled, and still his until the date. The one thing he must not be left guessing at, so it
       is the whole of what this state says — plus the way back, which is the same button that
       started it. */
    <div class="mt-4">
      <p class="m-0 text-[0.86rem] font-semibold">{d.subEnding.replace('{date}', endingOn.slice(0, 10))}</p>
      <p class="muted m-0 mt-1 text-[0.8rem]">{d.subEndingNote}</p>
      <button type="button" class="btn btn--accent btn--sm mt-3" id="sub-start" data-store={storeId}>{d.subResume}</button>
    </div>
  ) : paying && storeInPlan ? (
    <div class="mt-4">
      <div class="flex items-center justify-between gap-3 flex-wrap">
        <p class="m-0 text-[0.86rem] font-semibold">{d.subActive}</p>
        <div class="flex items-center gap-2">
          {/* ── The way off a plan, which a live shop did not have ──
              Owner, asked three times, most recently 2026-08-25: *"אם אדם כבר רץ עם חנות פעילה,
              איפה הוא משנה את המסלולים?"* The chooser lived inside the go-live step, which renders
              nothing once the shop is up — so from the moment a seller succeeded, his plan was
              fixed. The copy for this button was written on 2026-08-24 and never had a control
              under it.

              A toggle rather than four pills on show: this card's resting state is a statement that
              the subscription is running, and a chooser standing open under it reads as a decision
              being asked for. Pressed, it is the same pills and the same handler as the go-live
              step — `/api/seller/tier` patches the standing order at PayMe first and records the
              plan only if they accepted. */}
          <button type="button" class="btn btn--ghost btn--sm" id="sub-plan-toggle" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="sub-plan-picker">{d.subChangePlan}</button>
          <button type="button" class="btn btn--ghost btn--sm" id="sub-cancel">{d.subCancel}</button>
        </div>
      </div>

      {/* ── The payment method, on the one screen that never had one (owner, §9) ──
          There is no PAN to print — PayMe hold a token and we deliberately store no card digits —
          so the honest statement is that a card is on file and when it was given. The button is the
          same `#sub-card-replace` the armed state uses, opening the same form: two controls that
          did the same thing differently is how two paths to one outcome start disagreeing. */}
      <div class="mt-3 pt-3 border-t [border-color:var(--color-border)] flex items-center justify-between gap-3 flex-wrap">
        <div class="min-w-0">
          <p class="muted m-0 text-[0.76rem] font-medium">{d.subMethodTitle}</p>
          <p class="m-0 mt-0.5 text-[0.86rem] font-semibold">
            {cardSavedAt ? d.subMethodOnFileSince.replace('{date}', cardSavedAt.slice(0, 10)) : d.subMethodOnFile}
          </p>
        </div>
        <button type="button" class="btn btn--ghost btn--sm shrink-0" id="sub-card-replace" aria-controls="sub-card-fields" aria-expanded="false">{d.subCardReplace}</button>
      </div>

      <div class="!hidden mt-3" id="sub-plan-picker">
        <PlanPills storeId={storeId} tier={tier} planNames={planNames} showNote={false} d={d} />
        {/* The date, not "your next charge" — he is deciding whether to switch, and the one thing he
            wants to know is when it starts costing the new amount. PayMe's own next-charge string,
            the same one shown two rows above, so the two cannot disagree. */}
        <p class="muted m-0 mt-2 text-[0.78rem]">
          {subscription?.nextCharge
            ? d.subChangePlanDated.replace('{date}', subscription.nextCharge.slice(0, 10))
            : d.subChangePlanNote}
          {' '}<a href="/pricing?from=payouts">{d.glPlanCompare}</a>
        </p>
      </div>

      {/* ── The retention step ──
          Hidden until he presses cancel, and it is not a wall: the cancel button below is the same
          size as the alternatives beside it. What is offered is the two things that are genuinely
          cheaper than leaving and that he may not know are possible. */}
      <div class="!hidden mt-3 rounded-[var(--radius)] border [border-color:var(--color-border)] p-3" id="sub-cancel-panel">
        <p class="m-0 text-[0.86rem] font-semibold">{d.subCancelTitle}</p>
        {/* **With the DATE in it when we know one.** The rule alone — "until the end of the period
            you have paid for" — is a sentence a seller has to do arithmetic on at the one moment he
            is least inclined to. The date is PayMe's next-charge day, which is the same value shown
            a few rows above, so the two cannot disagree; without one (a subscription whose next
            date they have not returned) the sentence falls back to the rule, which is still true. */}
        <p class="muted m-0 mt-1 text-[0.82rem]">
          {subscription?.nextCharge
            ? d.subCancelWhenDated.replace('{date}', subscription.nextCharge.slice(0, 10))
            : d.subCancelWhen}
        </p>
        {/* ── What actually happens after, spelled out (owner, סשן א׳ §6, 2026-08-26) ──
            *"מה קורה אחרי ביטול המנוי? זה גם צריך הסבר ברור."* The line above says WHEN; this says
            what the shop and the data do — it comes down from the site at the end of the paid
            period, everything stays, and renewing brings it back. Three facts, because those are
            the three things a seller is actually afraid of. */}
        <p class="muted m-0 mt-2 text-[0.82rem]">{d.subCancelAfter}</p>

        {/* ── The question (owner, §6) ──
            *"למה אתה רוצה לבטל את המנוי? אפשרות לכתוב מלל חופשי."* Asked here rather than after the
            fact, because this is the one moment a seller is both motivated and specific.

            **Nothing here is required**, and the confirm button below does not wait for it: a
            cancellation gated on answering a survey is the dark pattern this panel is built not to
            be. `parseCancelReason` treats an empty answer as "he did not say", which is a real and
            permitted state (`lib/subscription-cancel.ts`).

            Buttons and a hidden input rather than radios: it is the same widget as the gender pair
            on the clearing form and the same one `subscription.ts` paints — a fill for the chosen
            one and `aria-pressed` beside it, never colour alone. */}
        <fieldset class="border-0 p-0 m-0 mt-3">
          <legend class="text-[0.84rem] font-semibold p-0 mb-2">{d.subCancelWhy}</legend>
          <input type="hidden" id="sub-cancel-reason" value="" />
          <div class="flex flex-wrap gap-2" id="sub-cancel-reasons">
            {CANCEL_REASONS.map((reason) => (
              <button type="button" class="btn btn--ghost btn--sm" data-reason={reason} aria-pressed="false">{reasonLabel(reason)}</button>
            ))}
          </div>
          <label class="field mt-3">
            <span class="text-[0.82rem]">{d.subCancelNote}</span>
            {/* `resize:none` is site-wide (Hard rules) and comes from the class; three rows is a
                paragraph without being an invitation to write an essay nobody will read. */}
            <textarea class="input" id="sub-cancel-note" rows="3" maxlength="600" placeholder={d.subCancelNotePlaceholder}></textarea>
          </label>
        </fieldset>

        <div class="flex flex-wrap items-center gap-2 mt-3">
          {/* The retention offer now DOES the thing instead of linking to a page about it. It was
              an `<a href="/pricing">` because a cheaper plan could not be chosen from this card at
              all — so the only honest offer was a marketing page, off the dashboard, with the
              cancel dialog abandoned behind it. With the chooser two rows up, "move to a cheaper
              plan" opens it. */}
          <button type="button" class="btn btn--ghost btn--sm" data-open-plans>{d.subCancelCheaper}</button>
          {lines.length > 1 && <span class="muted text-[0.8rem]">{d.subCancelOneStore}</span>}
          <button type="button" class="btn btn--sm" id="sub-cancel-confirm">{d.subCancelConfirm}</button>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  ) : cardOnFile ? (
    /* ── Card on file, nothing charged ──
       The state a seller spends PayMe's whole review in since 2026-08-24. It has to say three
       things and no more: that we hold the card, what will be taken, and WHEN — because the one
       thing that turns a saved card into a complaint is a charge whose date nobody named. */
    <div class="mt-4">
      <p class="m-0 text-[0.86rem] font-semibold">{d.subCardOnFile}</p>
      <p class="muted m-0 mt-1 text-[0.8rem]">
        {d.subCardWhen.replace('{amount}', formatAgorot(subscription?.priceAgorot ?? 0))}
      </p>
      {/* ── Replace, and REMOVE ──
          Quiet on purpose: an expired card between deciding and being approved is rare, and a
          prominent "change card" beside a saved one reads as doubt about whether it was saved.

          **Replace was a dead link until 2026-08-25.** It navigated to `?panel=payouts&card=1`, and
          `card=1` was a parameter nothing in the codebase read — so the page reloaded into exactly
          this state and the seller saw nothing happen (owner: *"החלפת כרטיס לא עובד"*). It reveals
          the real fields now, which render below in this state too.

          **Remove exists only here, and that is the whole of the rule.** This is a card on file
          with nothing charged — PayMe are still reviewing the business — and it was the one point
          in the flow a seller had committed with no way back short of deleting his shop. Once the
          subscription is RUNNING, taking the card away is cancellation and has its own screen with
          its own dates and its own retention step; a second control that quietly did the same thing
          differently is how two paths to one outcome start disagreeing. */}
      <div class="flex flex-wrap items-center gap-2 mt-3">
        <button type="button" class="btn btn--ghost btn--sm" id="sub-card-replace" aria-controls="sub-card-fields" aria-expanded="false">{d.subCardReplace}</button>
        <button type="button" class="btn btn--ghost btn--sm" id="sub-card-remove">{d.subCardRemove}</button>
      </div>
    </div>
  ) : paying && !storeInPlan ? (
    /* ── A shop being ADDED to a subscription that is already running ──
       He has a card PayMe are charging today, so there is nothing to type: one button, and the
       standing order grows by this shop's plan from the next charge. Falling through to the card
       fields would ask a paying seller to enter a card he has already given us, which is the one
       thing a second shop must not feel like (owner, 2026-08-25: *"אם מוכר פותח חנות חדשה וכבר יש
       לו חשבון סליקה, איך כל העניין הזה מופיע לו שם?"* — clearing is done, and so is the card). */
    <div class="mt-4">
      <button type="button" class="btn btn--accent btn--sm" id="sub-start" data-store={storeId}>{d.subAddStore}</button>
      <p class="muted m-0 mt-2 text-[0.78rem]">{d.subAddStoreNote.replace('{fee}', String(plan.monthlyFee))}</p>
    </div>
  ) : (
    <div class="mt-4">
      {created && !paying && <p class="m-0 mb-2 text-[0.84rem]">{d.subAwaitingPayment}</p>}

      {/* A paying seller opening a SECOND shop is not starting a subscription — he is adding a line
          to one that is already running, and the button has to say that or it reads as a second
          monthly charge appearing from nowhere. */}
      {/* The fallback route, shown only when the card fields could not be drawn — a deployment
          whose own merchant was opened without keeping its public key (`payment-payme.ts`). The
          script un-hides whichever of the two is real, so a seller never sees both. */}
      {/* ── The fallback route, and everything about it lives INSIDE it ──
          These two sentences used to sit outside this box, so they printed under the card fields as
          well — a seller typing his card on our page was told he was being taken to the processor's
          page, and the setup-fee line appeared twice on one screen (owner, 2026-08-25: *"מה הכוונה
          מעבירים אותך לדף התשלום? והנה זה בדיוק כפילות שדיברתי עליה"*). They are true only of this
          route, so they belong to it. */}
      <div class="!hidden" id="sub-hosted-fallback">
        <button type="button" class="btn btn--accent btn--sm" id="sub-start" data-store={storeId}>
          {created ? d.subResume : d.subStart}
        </button>
        {/* Said BEFORE the click, not after it: a redirect nobody was expecting is where a payment
            gets abandoned.

            **In the portfolio demonstration it is REPLACED, not joined** (`lib/demo-mode.ts`).
            There is no processor and no page to be taken to: `payme-demo.ts` answers
            `generate-subscription` active with no `sub_url`, so the handler falls through to its
            reload and the subscription is simply on. Leaving the sentence would make the button
            promise a redirect that visibly never happens — the one dead end this demonstration must
            not contain. The setup-fee line goes with it, for the same reason: nothing is charged. */}
        {demoMode
          ? <p class="muted m-0 mt-2 text-[0.78rem]">{d.subDemoCard}</p>
          : (
            <>
              <p class="muted m-0 mt-2 text-[0.78rem]">{d.subGoingToProcessor}</p>
              <p class="muted m-0 mt-1 text-[0.78rem]">{d.subSetupFee.replace('{amount}', String(SELLER_SETUP_FEE))}</p>
            </>
          )}
      </div>

    </div>
  )}
</section>
